  "query_preview": "(Today through Sunday)...\nIssued at 241 AM EDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nSurface analysis early this morning shows strong high pressure off \nthe Carolina coast, providing warm and humid southerly flow within \nthe lower levels. A frontal boundary was found from western MN \nacross NB to KS and the TX panhandle. Aloft, water vapor showed \nridging in place over upper midwest. a plume of tropical moisture \ncontinued to stream across the American SW to NB and MN before \ndiving SE to IN/OH and NY. This upper level moisture continued to \nproduce the high cloud cover across Central Indiana.\n\nToday and Tonight...\n\nMore dry weather is expected today and tonight as forecast soundings \nsuggest dry lower and middle levels with the warm but humid S-SW \nflow in place. Forecast soundings show the upper level moisture \ncontinuing to linger across the area through the day, but clearing \ntonight as the steering flow aloft becomes more westerly, pushing \nthose high clouds across the Great Lakes instead of into Indiana. \nThus a partly sunny day and a mostly clear night will be expected. \nAgain highs around 90, persistence, will be expected. Warmer temps \nwill be possible across southern parts of Central Indiana where \ncloud cover will be less substantial.\n\nTuesday and Wednesday...\n\nHot and humid weather looks in store for both Tuesday and Wednesday. \nStrong upper level high pressure will be stretching across the \nsouthern plains and Ohio Valley, keeping cloud cover and the upper \nflow across the Great Lakes. Forecast soundings on both days suggest \na very dry and very hot column, with 700mb temps near 12C. Surface \nhigh pressure anchored over the southeastern states will continue to \nprovide hot and humid southerly flow. 850mb temps near 22C will \nallow for highs in the mid 90s on Tuesday and Wednesday. Should \nthis stay the course, Heat advisories will be needed. \n\nThursday through Friday...\n\nWeak ridging within the upper flow is expected to build across the \nupper midwest, allowing NW flow to begin to develop across Central \nIndiana by Friday. Strong upper level high pressure will be centered \nover KS and MO. A weak wave is suggested to pass across northern \nIndiana on Thursday, followed by a stronger wave on Friday afternoon \ninto Friday Night.\n\nA weak frontal boundary will be in the vicinity of Central Indiana \non Thursday, passing south of our area on Friday Night. This along \nwith an expected hot, humid and unstable air mass in place will \ncontinue the need for at least chance pops during that time.\n\nSaturday and Sunday...\n\nThe strong upper level high pressure system is suggested to \nstrengthen and retrograde west on Saturday into Sunday, centering \nover western KS and eastern CO. This will result in a strong ridge \nover the CONUS, with monsoon like Pacific flow arriving over So Cal \nand NV before shooting north to Montana and Central CA before before \nturning quickly south the western Great Lakes into Indiana. Again, \nembedded waves within this flow are expected to ridge ride to \nIndiana, providing some upper support. Lee Side flow will provide \nsome subsidence across the eastern Great Lakes, resulting in a \nstronger surface high there, and should the earlier front make it \nfar south, cooler easterly flow arriving across Central Indiana for \nSaturday and Sunday. At the moment, forecast soundings show best \nsaturation on Saturday Night, with a cooler and dry sounding on \nSunday. Thus for now, pops will be needed on Saturday and Saturday \nNight, and confidence remains low for Sunday.",
